[{"id":"VRCE-5-39-1","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":1,"english":"Having been honoured by her (Sītā) in excellent words, the monkey proceeding, left that place, thinking.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.894,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039001","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"33821faab23f3aed72475a1293828f9a60a2b7fd13c912d700a11c4b112449b1","compact":"37a02731f25227c980195dc2e7febdb5c4d1dd55cdafc4f62dc2c48e2128aa79","nasal":"66f70524d04ef52edc4b5c60bf58696ff147ec005184fe9dad77cd790d99c3ed"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-2","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":2,"english":"A small work it is that remains. Beheld have I this dark-eyed lady. Passing by three means, I see that I shali have to resort to the fourth.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039002","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"35c61f80e1c6a3f2b212d09108ead9125e46ea2f3ad7daffd2539d0884c105b8","compact":"291c14ec5333f4138612fee7fd050463e358569b57ec396044791e084a0c3e58","nasal":"291c14ec5333f4138612fee7fd050463e358569b57ec396044791e084a0c3e58"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-3","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":3,"english":"No treaty with Rākşasa can answer the end. Neither can gifts prevail with the wealthy. Nor is it possible to sow dissensions among persons proud of their strength. Then, display of prowess appear to me applicable in this case.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.78,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039003","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3728230c92ca82cd3eeaa0107f80a5869e2767aa2f8d41c1e09dacfa0757ba6b","compact":"1beb8919dea459b1c7ad338fa031a00a0ab157b1fe79c60dc2865f8879b8a758","nasal":"1beb8919dea459b1c7ad338fa031a00a0ab157b1fe79c60dc2865f8879b8a758"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-4","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":4,"english":"Without resorting to prowess, way find I none for ensuring success in this matter. If the Rākşasa find their foremost heroes fallen in battle, they may temper their (martial ardour).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.663,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039004","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"99f344ef1e2596dd2faac12217d447a59dbe4ad3324bf775cb1c5086dce3eb1d","compact":"09b8411f393f6279cd936ceb3988a9f99757e4097358e5435eee6d36b2496262","nasal":"09b8411f393f6279cd936ceb3988a9f99757e4097358e5435eee6d36b2496262"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-5","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":5,"english":"He that, having compassed his (principal) purpose, accomplishes many more without marring the prime one, is entitled to act.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.701,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039005","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"144281e8063bdb42cae954830f44c2cd9340ee893b9e503d7e21935b3caa9375","compact":"cef698087acf783276dafa74edf056ff62e83bd1c3dd050e5cbf514621eadabe","nasal":"cef698087acf783276dafa74edf056ff62e83bd1c3dd050e5cbf514621eadabe"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-6","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":6,"english":"He that dose a small work, is certainly not a worker, he it is that know to bring about success in diverse ways, is along capable of securing the same. (The commentator explains this passage thus:\"He that by great pains accomplishes a small work, cannot be a great actor; but he that bring about his end in a variety of ways through the minimum of effort, in the actor.\")","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039006","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c1c455b0cad9316dc3129b5686771efb66f58b5e8982ccd7145f52877b8597e6","compact":"2392240d4cb9d02e7f02b5849abac87e3c9e14d9c74eb7dd484341a13b265008","nasal":"2392240d4cb9d02e7f02b5849abac87e3c9e14d9c74eb7dd484341a13b265008"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-7","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":7,"english":"Although my sole commission was to effect this much, (i.e. see Sita) yet if I repair to the abode of the lord of monkeys after having ascertained the strength of our ownselves and that of the foe in the field, then I shall have done the mandate of my master.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.737,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039007","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e12b7851631fdca0723bb946b252f51d1f63f5dae99db2d4e40dbc67419b3270","compact":"90d3392360de9277afc7003cd60b1cec8f8558c9a36d78fddb48406df6723ceb","nasal":"1e1219ee9fa3c4ae1e02fa9e424804a53bc3f9e9c5fe0ad1b0064658b3a8e86e"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-8","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":8,"english":"How can my arrival (at this place) be made to bring forth good fruit? How can I forcibly bring on engagement with the Rākşasas? And how can the Ten-necked one in battle be made to form a just estimate of the respective strength of myself and his forces?","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039008","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ffbb43a07c9e4d13b927e1675384258d73d704699ca7ff9792ac686a7dd499c2","compact":"d585c20e261eb6659263e7cc5c409a701c19e5a17f24d891a65065c70f4e68ad","nasal":"d585c20e261eb6659263e7cc5c409a701c19e5a17f24d891a65065c70f4e68ad"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-9","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":9,"english":"This grove of the fell (fiend) resembling Nandana itself, containing various trees and plants, and captivating to eye and heart, will i destroy; even as fire destroy a dry wood. This grove being ravaged, Rāvana shall get into a fury.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.702,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039009","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"ac5c9f5452253a1ebcb2af8a484fb8287b9f62a9a81243384288a0c1042c6d9e","compact":"b458d91c50ee7dfde00da4e4fba03bac30a966571f012a703050f9ceb324375a","nasal":"ad6a7e75ee1d7ce448267f5c1e4f268f5425ba652fd25909f2405d2b4a4a2a06"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-10","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":10,"english":"This grove of the fell (fiend) resembling Nandana itself, containing various trees and plants, and captivating to eye and heart, will i destroy; even as fire destroy a dry wood. This grove being ravaged, Rāvana shall get into a fury.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039010","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6cf499902c0999a339514070c7fa00ff04f7c3316f7ef22ff307ae973f2c6793","compact":"60b9d34e99e841d16d485317bda77b3e30127cbd4c8652fffef89f8556145a24","nasal":"60b9d34e99e841d16d485317bda77b3e30127cbd4c8652fffef89f8556145a24"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-11","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":11,"english":"Then the Raksasa monarch shall summon up an army consisting of herse, elephants, and mighty cars, equipped with tridents, iron axes and other arms, and mighty shall be the encounter that shall take place.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.898,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039011","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cc156a74e3cc4ca857c349435baf39cf72e878834e2e87ec7ea72d8229060d50","compact":"5e641991abb065ea71083b98d4c46746198e1ef9d9e1aa8f876b999be1e4a4ff","nasal":"5e641991abb065ea71083b98d4c46746198e1ef9d9e1aa8f876b999be1e4a4ff"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-12","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":12,"english":"And I with unpaired prowess battling with those terrific Rākşasa, and annihilating that host despatched by Rāvana, shall merrily journey to the abode of the monkey king.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.73,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039012","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6fee71de1f53933bb876af2c7ea4f42605931d25de0d7c3485796d6692bf10f3","compact":"44c938e5ff9fe9a50930852e36864aa69bc41fbe76a570705fbfd573b558a9cf","nasal":"c6f6d63b6c2fc4f6c007416498f8ac787a4d9417f1e029c39e68a3b22a46701f"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-13","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":13,"english":"Then furious like the Wind, the offspring of the Wind-god possessed of dreadful prowess set about knocking down the trees with impetuous violence.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.862,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039013","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"44c2963fa8d83e858df3f09955acf6f4738ae852c9b197433479faab646c6d3c","compact":"ff3c51c4ec03c9f07421157eb776aac1b425f85c93e0d2e2e9fc847ba48951de","nasal":"ff3c51c4ec03c9f07421157eb776aac1b425f85c93e0d2e2e9fc847ba48951de"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-14","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":14,"english":"Then that hero, Hanumān, devastated that garden of the damsels, resounding with the roars of mad elephants, and filled with diverse trees and shrubs.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.852,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039014","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c81e591ef704242f8f31dbebb9d134019d7a7ef443e7836d0c83ee9726480093","compact":"b7fdae516a825db9cd2c8c911b32b457b67c5b118a3cd6acb2565fe2caf52e2f","nasal":"3d54ea5c69b7e41df04284d003ca1cab3bc21f66281b9a5891d2d3884bfce723"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-15","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":15,"english":"And with its trees crushed and its tanks damaged, with its fair peaks shattered in fragments and its pools eloquent with the notes of birds, riven all over, with coppery and withered sprays cast about (in all directions), and with its trees and plants shorn of their liveliness, that wood was no longer beautiful, as if it had been burnt up by a forest-fire; and its plants resembled damsels with their apparel falling off.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.62,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039015","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"216277482ff29350e20c03c3c1a0f28f719d01350c85920202dd1fd602440fcb","compact":"76da0a73845ed32b183b0b0da15039bba2134ea2cb71533df2c1220c71f35bcb","nasal":"b8caa55d65aa814f86d176f1708522fbd13204713df50d8631a7d6ae7a15f133"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-16","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":16,"english":"And that magnificent wood, with its arbours and its picture-galleries destroyed, and its ferocious animals, beasts and birds crying in distressful accents, and its rocky structures and other mansions broken down, was bereft of its loveliness.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.33,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039016","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a0fdf4468c996f2dc6fa327353073d2adf21e5d1e68fc1c1007de9d0fb8f0ca4","compact":"2e41bdf1660b011d0dc70c05b8383277fd3c392703f3aca4178f2e4705367724","nasal":"2e41bdf1660b011d0dc70c05b8383277fd3c392703f3aca4178f2e4705367724"}},{"id":"VRCE-5-39-17","kanda":5,"kandaId":"sundara","sarga":39,"verse":17,"english":"Then having done what was wondrous disagreeable to the mind of the magnanimous lord of the world, that monkey desirous of coping alone with a vast host, stood by the main entrance, flaming in effulgence.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.745,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-5-41-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"5039017","sanskritFile":"Ram05.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"51087c6d2cd0798bd9814619404fb260814c0e14dbbf77a43295c6ce7832196a","compact":"a3fb39c3d7b836244899d956f68bda17eea2044bf780340912704f67ca012fea","nasal":"a3fb39c3d7b836244899d956f68bda17eea2044bf780340912704f67ca012fea"}}]
